    "q-157235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q157235",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.",
      "worked_solution": "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-4-2 \\\\ 9+5-14 \\\\ -3+3+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
