    "q-157238": {
      "dom_id": "step-q157238",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.",
      "worked_solution": "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-8+6 \\\\ -1+10-9 \\\\ 7+2-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
